          "text": "The pattern is best indexed as a long-period, approximately hexagonal Lu polytype:\n\n- \\(a \\approx 3.604\\) \u00c5\n- \\(c \\approx 48.38\\) \u00c5\n- 18 Lu layers per cell, \\(V/Z_{\\rm atom}\\approx30.2\\) \u00c5\u00b3\n- approximate 18H-type stacking\n\nThe diagnostic \\(00l\\) series is approximately:\n\n| 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) | Index |\n|---:|:---|\n| 10.99 | 006 |\n| 18.28 | 00 10 |\n| 33.29 | 00 18 |\n| 45.06 | 00 24 |\n| 57.16 | 00 30 |\n| 69.9\u201370.2 | 00 36 |\n| 83.69 | 00 42 |\n\nThe strong basal reflections near 28.8\u00b0, 50.65\u00b0, and 59.1\u00b0 are consistent with approximately 100, 110, and 200. The non-fundamental intensities require a relaxed 18-layer stacking superstructure rather than ordinary hcp Lu or ideal 9R Sm-type Lu.\n\nPowder positions establish the long-period cell robustly, but the exact stacking sequence and layer displacements are not unique from this pattern alone. The following is a lower-symmetry commensurate representative consistent with the indexed cell and density.\n\n```cif\ndata_Lu_18_layer_primary\n_audit_creation_method\n;Indexed from the supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern.\nCommensurate 18-layer representative; layer coordinates are an\napproximate stacking model rather than a full Rietveld refinement.\n;\n_chemical_name_common                 '18-layer hexagonal Lu polytype'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'Lu18'\n_chemical_formula_structural          'Lu'\n_cell_formula_units_Z                  1\n\n_cell_length_a                         3.6040\n_cell_length_b                         3.6040\n_cell_length_c                         48.3800\n_cell_angle_alpha                      90\n_cell_angle_beta                       90\n_cell_angle_gamma                      120\n_cell_volume                           544.4\n\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt              'P 1'\n_space_group_IT_number                 1\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1 'x,y,z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_B_iso_or_equiv\nLu01 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.000000 1.0 0.50\nLu02 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.053000 1.0 0.50\nLu03 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.112500 1.0 0.50\nLu04 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.166000 1.0 0.50\nLu05 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.223500 1.0 0.50\nLu06 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.276000 1.0 0.50\nLu07 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.334500 1.0 0.50\nLu08 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.388000 1.0 0.50\nLu09 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.445000 1.0 0.50\nLu10 Lu 0.666667 0.333333 0.500000 1.0 0.50\nLu11 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.553000 1.0 0.50\nLu12 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.612500 1.0 0.50\nLu13 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.666000 1.0 0.50\nLu14 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.723500 1.0 0.50\nLu15 Lu 0.000000 0.000000 0.776000 1.0 0.50\nLu16 Lu 0.666667 0.333333 0.834500 1.0 0.50\nLu17 Lu 0.333333 0.666667 0.888000 1.0 0.50\nLu18 Lu 0.666667 0.333333 0.945000 1.0 0.50\n```\n\nOrdinary hcp Lu and the 9R Sm-type structure are not satisfactory single-phase alternatives: hcp cannot generate the low-angle superlattice peaks, while rhombohedral 9R symmetry forbids the strong reflection at 18.28\u00b0 that indexes as \\(00\\,10\\) in the doubled 18-layer cell.",
